<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.11.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00248</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.11.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>01c2c7f5-4625-4cf0-9296-edc141956bcf</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
      <para>For numbers 1 – 2, use the graph below:</para>
      <figure>
        <media alt="This figure a shows a straight line graphed on the x y-coordinate plane. The x and y axes run from negative 10 to 10. The line goes through the points (negative 6, negative 8), (negative 4, negative 6), (negative 2, negative 4), (0, negative 2), (2, 0), (4, 2), (6, 4), (8, 6).">
          <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_13_10_01.jpg"/>
        </media>
      </figure>
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <exercise id="_183419ea-c11a-4339-aadb-d9f6fddd56ed" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>What are the coordinates of the \(x\)-intercept of the graph?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The graph crosses the \(x\)-axis at \((0,2)\).</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.11.2. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is \((2,0)\). </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_0ff195d9-131c-40b6-9d54-694f267a6025" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>What are the coordinates of the \(y\)-intercept of the graph?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The graph crosses the \(y\)-axis at \((0,-2)\).</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.11.2. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is \((0,-2)\). </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_328040c7-4577-4c54-ba53-f3023e8a8ae7" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>For numbers 3 – 4, use the graph below:</para>
            <figure>
              <media alt="This figure a shows a straight line graphed on the x y-coordinate plane. The x and y axes run from negative 10 to 10. The line goes through the points (negative 6, 6), (negative 3, 4), (0, 2), (3, 0), (6, negative 2), and (9, negative 4).">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_13_10_02.jpg"/>
              </media>
            </figure>
            <para>What are the coordinates of the \(x\)-intercept of the graph?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The graph crosses the \(x\)-axis at (3,0).</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.11.2. </para>
            <para>The graph crosses the \(x\)-axis at \((3,0)\). </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="d9f6c4de-fe6a-4f3e-8663-849afb187a67" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>What are the coordinates of the \(y\)-intercept of the graph?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The graph crosses the \(y\)-axis at \((0,2)\).</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.11.2. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is \((0,2)\). </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_3adaa38d-78ca-4063-bee4-3133a26edf43" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>What are the coordinates of the \(x\)-intercept of the equation \(3x + y = 12\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! When \(y = 0, 3x + 0 = 12\) becomes \(x = 4\).</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.11.2. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is \((4,0)\). </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_99318492-7d7c-491b-b4ea-a460dfe1073b" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>What are the coordinates of the \(y\)-intercept of the equation \(3x + y = 12\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! When \(x = 0, 3(0) + y = 12\) becomes \(y = 12\).</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.11.2. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is \((0,12)\). </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="b15f1dc1-aa05-4cbf-b354-2c2c6129903f" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>What is the slope of the line \(3x- 2y=6\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The slope is \(\frac{3}{2}\), calculated from the equation in standard form.</para>
            <para>Try again. Remember the formula for slope in standard form. Use the Equation Editor to express your answer.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is \(\frac{3}{2}\). The slope is found by rearranging the equation into slope-intercept form and identifying the coefficient of \(x\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_8179854f-8533-4972-b9f9-094d93ae10f2" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
            <para>What is the \(y\)-intercept of \(y = 6x + 2\)? </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Compare your answer:
        In \(y = mx + b\), the \(b\) is the \(y\)-intercept. In this equation, \(b = 2\).
      
        In \(y = mx + b\), the \(b\) is the \(y\)-intercept. In this equation, \(b = 2\).
      </para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.11.2. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is \((0, 2)\). In \(y = mx + b\), the \(b\) is the \(y\)-intercept. In this equation, \(b = 2\). </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="fcec07ea-a807-4295-af28-e0b61a16035d" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>What is the \(y\)-intercept of \(10x + 5y = 30\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Compare your answer:
        Solve for \(y\).
        Solve for \(y\).
        \(10x - 10x + 5y = 30 - 10x\)
        \(10x - 10x + 5y = 30 - 10x\)
        \(5y = -10x + 30\)
        \(5y = -10x + 30\)\(\frac{5y}{5}=\frac{-10x +30}{5}\)\(\frac{5y}{5}=\frac{-10x +30}{5}\)
        \(y = -2x + 6\)
        \(y = -2x + 6\)
        In \(y = mx + b\), the \(b\) is the \(y\)-intercept. In this equation, \(b = 6\).
      
        In \(y = mx + b\), the \(b\) is the \(y\)-intercept. In this equation, \(b = 6\).
      </para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.11.2. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is \((0, 6)\). Solve for \(y\).Solve for \(y\).
        \(10x - 10x + 5y = 30 - 10x\)
        \(10x - 10x + 5y = 30 - 10x\)
        \(5y = -10x + 30\)
        \(5y = -10x + 30\)\(\frac{5y}{5}=\frac{-10x +30}{5}\)\(\frac{5y}{5}=\frac{-10x +30}{5}\)
        \(y = -2x + 6\)
        \(y = -2x + 6\)
        In \(y = mx + b\), the \(b\) is the \(y\)-intercept. In this equation, \(b = 6\). 
        In \(y = mx + b\), the \(b\) is the \(y\)-intercept. In this equation, \(b = 6\). </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_5c1e1577-2cff-4350-8771-d6abb4e818c3" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Which graph represents the equation \( 12=3x+4y \)? Explain how you know.</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="">
                  <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_13_10_03.png"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="">
                  <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_13_10_04.png"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="">
                  <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_13_10_05.png"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>a</para>
            <para>b</para>
            <para>c</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Graph a, because the intercepts are \((4, 0)\) and \((0,3)\).</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.11.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is Graph \(a\), because the intercepts are \((4, 0)\) and \((0,3)\). </para>
            <para>When \(x = 0\), \(12 = 3(0) + 4y\) gives \(y = 3\). The \(y\)-intercept is at (0, 3). When \(y = 0\), \(12 = 3x + 4(0)\) gives \(x = 4\). The \(x\)-intercept is (4, 0). Graph \(a\) has these intercepts.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
